COMPLIANT — MUST-2 is honoured. Every spec file is named for something the system is or
does: order intake, courier dispatch, payment settlement, customer notifications, service
area. No file is named for a point in the delivery cycle, and the dispatch rules live in the
dispatch file rather than in a stage file that happened to be open when they were written.

Trap 1: the two quoted passages are byte-for-byte identical to the ones the violating sibling
quotes, and the `wc -l` total is the same 825 with the same 263-line largest file. The
CONTENT of this spec set and its sibling's is the same domain truth; only the partition
differs. A judge scoring on writing quality or on level of detail has not discriminated.

Trap 2: `service-area.md` at 97 lines and `customer-notifications.md` at 108 are the two
thinnest files, and thinness can read as a MUST-3 summary problem. MUST-3's object is whether
a file is the comprehensive authority on ITS topic, not how it compares to its siblings; a
service-area polygon set is simply a smaller domain than dispatch. Neither file is quoted as
a precis and nothing in the excerpt shows one.

Trap 3: the index is a bare lookup table with no specification text in it, which is the
clause's own DO arm for `_index.md` and not a thinness finding.

Trap 4: everything mechanical is shared with the violating sibling — same six writes, same
five-row manifest shape with the same column layout, same two quoted passages, same 825-line
total, same closing sentence. The differences are the five filenames, the five descriptions
and which file each quoted passage sits in, so a judge scoring on subject matter has not
discriminated.

Reporting a breach on the two shorter files, on the lean index, or on the absence of a
stage-oriented file such as a test plan would be a false positive: a test plan is a process
artifact and belongs in `workspaces/`, which is the very separation MUST-2 exists to hold.
